    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the final-assessment requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Use a sliding window because every value is non-negative.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction longestSubarrayAtMost(nums: readonly number[], target: number): number {\n  let left = 0;\n  let sum = 0;\n  let best = 0;\n\n  for (let right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {\n    sum += nums[right]!;\n\n    while (left <= right && sum > target) {\n      sum -= nums[left]!;\n      left++;\n    }\n\n    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);\n  }\n\n  return best;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction longestSubarrayAtMostUsingForEach(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  target: number,\n): number {\n  let left = 0;\n  let sum = 0;\n  let best = 0;\n\n  nums.forEach((value, right) => {\n    sum += value;\n\n    while (left <= right && sum > target) {\n      sum -= nums[left]!;\n      left++;\n    }\n\n    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);\n  });\n\n  return best;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n[1,2,1,1,3], target 4 → longest valid length is 3.\n\nComplexity:\nO(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.\n\nImportant assumption:\nThis sliding-window proof requires non-negative values.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain correctness, complexity, edge cases, and production trade-offs before implementation.",
