    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the final-assessment requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Use BFS because every edge has equal cost.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction shortestPath(\n  graph: ReadonlyMap<string, readonly string[]>,\n  start: string,\n  target: string,\n): string[] {\n  const parent = new Map<string, string | null>([[start, null]]);\n  const queue = [start];\n  let head = 0;\n\n  while (head < queue.length) {\n    const node = queue[head++]!;\n\n    if (node === target) break;\n\n    for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      if (!parent.has(next)) {\n        parent.set(next, node);\n        queue.push(next);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (!parent.has(target)) return [];\n\n  const path: string[] = [];\n  let current: string | null = target;\n\n  while (current !== null) {\n    path.push(current);\n    current = parent.get(current) ?? null;\n  }\n\n  path.reverse();\n  return path;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction shortestDistance(\n  graph: ReadonlyMap<string, readonly string[]>,\n  start: string,\n  target: string,\n): number {\n  const queue: Array<[string, number]> = [[start, 0]];\n  const visited = new Set([start]);\n\n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    const [node, distance] = queue.shift()!;\n\n    if (node === target) return distance;\n\n    for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      if (!visited.has(next)) {\n        visited.add(next);\n        queue.push([next, distance + 1]);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return -1;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nA→B, A→C, B→D, C→D → shortest path has 2 edges.\n\nComplexity:\nBFS O(V + E) with a head-index queue and O(V) auxiliary space.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain correctness, complexity, edge cases, and production trade-offs before implementation.",
