    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the final-assessment requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Create a closure containing a cache.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction memoize(\n  fn: (value: number) => number,\n): (value: number) => number {\n  const cache = new Map<number, number>();\n\n  return (value: number): number => {\n    if (cache.has(value)) return cache.get(value)!;\n\n    const result = fn(value);\n    cache.set(value, result);\n    return result;\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction memoizeWithMap(\n  fn: (value: number) => number,\n): (value: number) => number {\n  const cache = new Map<number, number>();\n\n  return (value) => {\n    if (!cache.has(value)) cache.set(value, fn(value));\n    return cache.get(value)!;\n  };\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nconst square = memoize((value) => value * value);\nsquare(5) calculates; the second square(5) uses the cache.\n\nComplexity:\nHit O(1) average plus no recomputation. Memory O(number of cached keys).\n\nImportant:\nMemoization assumes the cached result is valid for the key.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain correctness, complexity, edge cases, and production trade-offs before implementation.",
