    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the final-assessment requirement.\n\nStep 1 — Track request count and window start per key.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface WindowState {\n  start: number;\n  count: number;\n}\n\nclass FixedWindowRateLimiter {\n  private readonly windows = new Map<string, WindowState>();\n\n  constructor(\n    private readonly limit: number,\n    private readonly windowMs: number,\n  ) {}\n\n  allow(key: string, now = Date.now()): boolean {\n    const current = this.windows.get(key);\n\n    if (!current || now - current.start >= this.windowMs) {\n      this.windows.set(key, { start: now, count: 1 });\n      return true;\n    }\n\n    if (current.count >= this.limit) return false;\n\n    current.count++;\n    return true;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-style timestamp scan:\n```ts\nfunction canRequest(\n  timestamps: readonly number[],\n  now: number,\n  windowMs: number,\n  limit: number,\n): boolean {\n  const recent = timestamps.filter(\n    (timestamp) => now - timestamp < windowMs,\n  );\n\n  return recent.length < limit;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nlimit=3 per 1000ms; requests at 100,200,300 pass; 400 is rejected; 1200 starts a new window.\n\nComplexity:\nMap-based check O(1) average.\nTimestamp filtering O(n).\n\nSystem-design takeaway:\nClient-side limiting is not authoritative security enforcement; distributed enforcement belongs on the server.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain correctness, complexity, edge cases, and production trade-offs before implementation.",
